<h2>Tracking maintenance requests through a CRM pipeline</h2>

<p>Maintenance is where property managers either shine or lose credibility. A tenant reports a leaking tap. If you respond quickly, organise a plumber, and follow up to confirm the job is done, that tenant trusts you. If the request disappears into an email thread and nothing happens for two weeks, that tenant starts looking for a new property manager.</p>

<p>A CRM pipeline built for maintenance requests gives you visibility over every job from start to finish. Here is a pipeline structure that works well:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>New request.</strong> The tenant submits a maintenance request through a form (linked in their welcome email or accessible via a saved link). The request creates a new record in your CRM with the tenant's details, the property, a description of the issue, and any photos they have attached.</li>
  <li><strong>Under review.</strong> You assess the request. Is it urgent? Does the owner need to approve the cost? Does it fall under routine maintenance or is it something the tenant is responsible for?</li>
  <li><strong>Approved and assigned.</strong> Once approved, you assign it to a tradesperson. An automated notification goes to the tradesperson with the job details. Another goes to the tenant confirming that the issue is being addressed and who will be in contact.</li>
  <li><strong>In progress.</strong> The tradesperson is working on it. You can log updates here, and the tenant receives a status update if the job extends beyond the expected timeline.</li>
  <li><strong>Completed.</strong> The job is done. The tenant receives an automated message confirming completion and asking them to report any further issues. The owner receives a summary of what was done and the cost.</li>
</ul>

<p>This pipeline approach means nothing gets lost. You can see at a glance how many open maintenance requests you have, which ones are overdue, and which properties are generating the most issues. It is the same principle behind <a href="/blog/customer-follow-up-system">building a follow-up system</a> for any business. Every interaction gets tracked, and nothing slips through the cracks.</p>

