<h2>Setting up your property management CRM</h2>

<p>You do not need to build everything at once. Start with the areas that cause you the most pain, and expand from there. Here is a practical order of priority:</p>

<p><strong>Step one: centralise your contacts.</strong> Get every tenant, owner, and tradesperson into one system with proper tags. Tag tenants by property, by lease status (active, vacating, prospective), and by any relevant notes. Tag owners by their properties. Tag tradespeople by their trade and area of service.</p>

<p><strong>Step two: build your maintenance pipeline.</strong> This is usually the biggest source of dropped balls. Set up the pipeline stages described above, create a maintenance request form, and link it in every tenant's welcome email. Train your tenants to submit requests through the form instead of texting you at 10pm.</p>

<p><strong>Step three: automate your rent reminders.</strong> Set up SMS reminders one day before rent is due and automated follow-ups for late payments. This alone will save you hours each month and reduce the awkwardness of chasing payments manually.</p>

<p><strong>Step four: automate lease renewals.</strong> Build a simple workflow that checks lease end dates and triggers the renewal sequence at 90, 60, and 30 days. Include the renewal terms, instructions for the tenant, and a clear deadline for their response.</p>

<p><strong>Step five: build your welcome sequence.</strong> Create the onboarding emails that fire when a new tenant moves in. Do this once and every future tenant gets the same professional experience without you writing a single additional email.</p>

<p>Each of these steps builds on the last. By the time you have all five in place, you have a system that handles the bulk of your routine communication automatically. You are not spending your evenings typing reminder emails. You are spending your time on strategy, growth, and the parts of property management that actually require your expertise.</p>

<h2>What to look for in a property management CRM</h2>

<p>Not every CRM is built for property management, but a flexible platform can be configured to fit your workflow perfectly. Here is what matters most:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Custom pipelines.</strong> You need the ability to create pipelines for different processes. One for maintenance, one for lease renewals, one for vacancies, one for tenant applications. If the CRM only gives you a single sales pipeline, it is not going to cut it.</li>
  <li><strong>Automation triggers based on dates.</strong> Lease renewals, inspection schedules, and rent reminders all depend on dates. Your CRM needs to trigger actions based on custom date fields, not just manual inputs.</li>
  <li><strong>Multi-channel communication.</strong> Tenants want to be reached where they are. Some prefer email. Others respond faster to SMS. The best setup uses both, just like <a href="/blog/reduce-no-shows">appointment reminder systems that combine SMS and email</a> to maximise response rates.</li>
  <li><strong>Contact tagging and segmentation.</strong> You need to be able to segment by property, by owner, by lease status, and by tenant type. This lets you send targeted communications instead of blasting everyone with the same message.</li>
  <li><strong>Reporting.</strong> How many open maintenance requests do you have? What is your average response time? Which properties generate the most issues? A good CRM gives you these answers without exporting data to a spreadsheet.</li>
</ul>

<p>The best property management CRM is one you actually use every day. It should simplify your workflow, not add complexity. If setting it up feels like building a spaceship, it is probably the wrong tool.</p>

